Date: Wed,  3 Mar 93 10:28:36 PST
From: hkhenson@cup.portal.com
Subject: POLI/RELIG: Church and State

Tom Morrow writes:  

>On further consideration, I've decided that statism is the worse of the
>two.  
>
>Why?  Because so long as I remain free to control my own person and
>property it matters little to me what religious people say.  I can still
>say, hear, and think what I want.  But once statists start to interfere
>with the tools that are necessary for me to maintain my liberty of
>conscience, they make it hard or impossible for me to counteract religion or
>other baneful ideologies.

The problem is more complex.  It is a historical (and current) fact 
that any religion which becomes dominate uses the power of the state 
to suppress the competition.  Christianity has certainly done that, 
and does it to this day in places where one sect has sufficient 
control.  The most extreme example in the last century would be 
communism, which, if it is not finally classed as a religion, is 
certainly in a class which included religions and is directly 
competitive with them for mind space.  

What seems to be the most Extropian approach to keeping one religion 
from becoming dominate is to have a whole ecosystem of them competing, 
and to try to limit recruitment into the worst of them.  (I was once 
involved in taking some risk to making things difficult for a splinter 
cult off of communism which has Lyndon LaRouche as its head.)  Most 
main stream religions (in western culture) have adapted to an effect 
of the memetic ecosystem by supporting a limited amount of "fair 
competition" via the meta meme of tolerance, and (to some extent) 
separating church and state.  There is material here for a PhD thesis 
at least. 

Keith Henson

