>bankrupt).  Anyone with money lent out to it looses it.
>
>I've always wondered how bankruptcy would be handled in a PPL world, and
>now I think I know (one flavor) . . . 

I agree that the case of the banker skipping town is "one flavor"--after
all, we see this today with BCCI, collapsing S & Ls, Banco Nazionale del
Lavoro, the Vatican Bank, and so on. But it's not the only flavor,
especially not with robust reputation systems and PPL or market-oriented
law.

PPL or customary law is actually very widespread--international dealings,
your dealing with you friends and family, etc. (I'm about 75% done with a
big essay, "Customary Law is all Around Us," which makes the point that PPL
or customary law is actually the _norm_ for most of our interactions in the
world, and is not some lofty goal for the distant future. I'll send it out
to the list as soon as I can.)

It is unlikely you'll deposit your hard-earned money with some crypto
entity that could vanish overnight, any more than you'd give your cash to
some stranger on the street for safekeeping. True, some scams will develop,
some people will be burned--things like that happen in a free society.

Crypto banks, for example, will likely have backings from insurance funds
and bonding agencies. The precise form is hard to accurately predict.
"Alice's Crypto Bank" may charge some amount for deposits and services,
some of which is then paid to bonding or escrow agencies (themselves using
crypto methods, of course).

Before you leave your money with Alice's Crypto Bank, you'll want to see
some assurances that Alice won't skip town. Positive reputations and
bonding entities perform this function.

There's a lot more to say.

-Tim
