Subject: PPL: extracting compensation

> If prostitution, surrogate child bearing, selling body parts for
> cash, etc. is all legal (and, as we all know, they should be), in
> short, if everything has a market value, cannot a court order
> such things sold to repay a debt or to recompense a victim?

Sure, why not?

In my view, the best way to structure criminal justice is not around
the idea of retribution, but rather restitution. If you transgress
against someone, you owe a debt to them or their estate. If you cannot
pay it, they should hold a contract on your labour that allows them to
collect. (A sort of indentured servitude). If they choose to sell your
contract, as most will, who is the criminal to complain? And if they
sell your contract to a brothel or if you propose to sell your organs
to pay off the debt, well that's just another way to settle.

Some will object to this, no doubt. As for my part, it seems to me that
the milk of human kindness is in short supply these days and can't be
wasted on criminals.

